commands/EXTRAVERSION


commands/EXTRAVERSION                                         commands/EXTRAVERSION

   NAME
	EXTRAVERSION - Find the extra version string (non-Amiga version).

   VERSION
	@VERSION@ (@DATE@)

   FORMAT
	EXTRAVERSION [<library|script|file>] [FULL] [VSTRING]
	             [VER=VERSTAG] [VERIFY] [HELP]

   TEMPLATE
	NAME,FULL/S,HELP/S,VSTRING/S,VER=VERSTAG/S,VERIFY/S

   PATH
	C:EXTRAVERSION

   FUNCTION
	EXTRAVERSION finds the extra version string of a library, command,
	script or any other file. The extra version string holds the version
	of the original, non-Amiga software (for example "0.3-Alpha_3" or
	"2.45.0-rc1"), which often does not fit the Amiga version string.
	It is a complement to the Amiga version string, never a replacement:
	the file must still have a normal $VER: string.

	EXTRAVERSION with no <library|script|file> argument prints its own
	name and extra version.

	By default, only the name and the extra version are printed. The
	FULL option prints the date as well, and the comment on a second
	line when there is one.

	The VSTRING option prints the whole extra version string, starting
	with $EXTVER:. If the file has no extra version string, an empty
	line is printed instead. This is useful in scripts.

	The VER (or VERSTAG) option reads the normal $VER: string of the
	file instead of the $EXTVER: string. All other options work the
	same way.

	The VERIFY option prints the string as it was found, followed by
	each part of it: name, version, date and comment.

	The HELP option prints a short help text.

	When the file has no extra version string, EXTRAVERSION prints
	"Could not find extra version information" and sets the return
	code to 20 (FAIL), like VERSION does. With VSTRING alone the return
	code stays 0.

   THE EXTRA VERSION STRING
	To add an extra version string to your program, put this string of
	characters in it:

	    "\0$EXTVER: Program_Name ExtraVersionString (dd.mm.yyyy) Comment\0"

	Program_Name        - name of your program, one word (use _ for
	                      spaces)
	ExtraVersionString  - any non-Amiga version string (use _ for
	                      spaces)
	dd.mm.yyyy          - day, month and year with four digits
	Comment             - optional, just for readability

	In a script, put it in a comment line:

	    ; $EXTVER: My_Script 0.3-Alpha_3 (12.5.2014) First test

	In C, keep the string in the program even when it is not used:

	    static const char extver[] __attribute__((used)) =
	        "\0$EXTVER: Program_Name 1.0.0-rc1 (20.11.2026)";

	The string starts after "$EXTVER:" (upper case, as $VER:) and ends
	at a NUL, at the end of the line or at the end of the file. The name
	is the first word, the version is the rest up to the "(" of the
	date, and the comment follows the ")". A $EXTVER: without a name, a
	version or a date does not count, and the search goes on, so a
	program that only searches for "$EXTVER:" does not confuse
	EXTRAVERSION.

   EXAMPLES
	For the script above, saved as S:My_Script:

	    1> EXTRAVERSION
	    ExtraVersion @VERSION@

	    1> EXTRAVERSION S:My_Script
	    My_Script 0.3-Alpha_3

	    1> EXTRAVERSION S:My_Script FULL
	    My_Script 0.3-Alpha_3 (12.5.2014)
	    First test

	    1> EXTRAVERSION S:My_Script VSTRING
	    $EXTVER: My_Script 0.3-Alpha_3 (12.5.2014) First test

	    1> EXTRAVERSION C:EXTRAVERSION VER VSTRING
	    $VER: ExtraVersion @VERSION@ (@DATE@)

   NOTES
	<library|script|file> does not support pattern matching.

	The Amiga version string is still required. ExtraVersion only
	complements it when the original version cannot be written as an
	Amiga version.revision number.

   SEE ALSO
	VERSION
